Re the either-or: it is the second one, and I would like it written down in=
 those terms rather than left to whoever touches the check next. For me it =
only counts as a stop when the marker is the tail end of what the model sai=
d. If the marker turns up mid-paragraph, the model is obviously still going=
, and the only correct thing for the reader to do is carry on accumulating =
and leave the marker in the text as ordinary content. The two transcripts G=
ideon found are exactly that case and nothing else needs to be said about t=
hem =E2=80=94 the model was explaining our format, the marker had prose aft=
er it, so it was never a stop.

That said, I want to be careful about what "tail end" means while a stream =
is still open, because that is where I expect this to be got wrong. It mean=
s the tail end of the model's output, not the tail end of whatever chunk ha=
ppens to have arrived. A marker landing at the end of one chunk with three =
more chunks behind it is mid-paragraph in every sense that matters to us; w=
e simply have not seen the rest yet. So the position test only has an answe=
r once the stream has closed, and until then the reader has nothing to deci=
de. If I had to guess, that is the part that produced the original loose ch=
eck in the first place =E2=80=94 somebody wanted an answer per chunk and th=
e only test that gives one per chunk is the one that ignores position entir=
ely. I wrote a fair amount of that reader on a late night and I would not s=
wear it was reasoned about any harder than that at the time.

